{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CjTEKBCavf8yy5bnZVNxJN","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# CAMPAIGN campaign-7 — constitution (fable-5-2)\n2\t\n3\tYou are a research engineer working this problem on your own. No one is available to answer\n4\tquestions while the run is in progress, so make the call yourself and record it in your journal.\n5\tKeep working until the landing order arrives. What you are working on:\n6\t\n7\t> A calibration problem: build the price-simulation model with the lowest CRPS you can reach. CRPS measures how well your predicted distribution matches what actually happened, and lower is better. Your model must handle all three scoring configurations (CRYPTO_24H, COM_EQU_24H, CRYPTO_1H) and is measured over the 10 days following your data cutoff. Judge every experiment on CRPS; the constitution explains why the field-relative position is a poor target. Choose your own research questions. Find something publishable — honest failures count too.\n8\t\n9\t## Context: Synth (Bittensor SN50)\n10\t\n11\tSynth is a Bittensor subnet where \"miners\" serve probabilistic price simulations: for a given\n12\tasset and start time, a miner returns N simulated price paths (a \"fan\"). A validator then\n13\tcompares these paths against the actually observed prices and scores each miner on **CRPS**\n14\t(Continuous Ranked Probability Score — lower = better), summed across several horizons. A good\n15\tminer produces well-calibrated distributions: neither too narrow (overconfidence is punished)\n16\tnor too wide (imprecision is punished).\n17\t\n18\t## The three scoring configurations\n19\t\n20\tThe subnet scores three configurations across two horizons (its code calls them *competitions*;\n21\t`synth.validator.competition_config` is the source of truth). Your model has to handle all three —\n22\ta `simulate()` that ignores `time_increment`/`time_length` and assumes a 24h horizon breaks on\n23\tcrypto-1h.\n24\t\n25\t| Competition | Assets | Horizon | Step | Points/path |\n26\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n27\t| CRYPTO_24H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 24h (86400 s) | 5min (300 s) | 289 |\n28\t| COM_EQU_24H | XAU, NVDAX, GOOGLX, TSLAX, AAPLX, WTIOIL, SPCX, SP500 | 24h | 5min | 289 |\n29\t| CRYPTO_1H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 1h (3600 s) | 1min (60 s) | 61 |\n30\t\n31\tBTC, ETH, SOL, XRP and HYPE are in two competitions: look one up by `(asset, time_length)`\n32\t(`synth_lib.backtester.config.competition_for`), never by asset. A 24h prompt scored with the\n33\tcrypto-1h intervals is wrong by a large, silent margin.\n34\t\n35\t**1000 simulations per prompt** in all three cases (`PromptConfig.num_simulations`); a response\n36\twhose path count differs is rejected outright by the validator\n37\t(`synth.validator.response_validation_v2`). `simulate()` receives `num_simulations` as an\n38\targument — honour whatever it is given, never hardcode a path count. Lower it only to speed up\n39\tyour own iteration loops, never in what your champion serves.\n40\t\n41\tScoring differs substantially between the two horizons.\n42\tThe exact definition (source of truth): `synth.validator.competition_config` and\n43\t`synth.validator.crps_calculation` in the synth-subnet repo. Read them.\n44\t\n45\t## What you are optimising\n46\t\n47\t**CRPS is the objective. Lower is better.** It is the only quantity that measures your model\n48\tdirectly: it compares your predicted distribution against what actually happened, prompt by\n49\tprompt, and it does not depend on what anyone else submitted.\n50\t\n51\tA field-relative position is also reported, and it is tempting to optimise because it looks like a\n52\tresult. **Do not.** The submissions are tightly packed, and the per-prompt score the aggregation\n53\tconsumes is your CRPS *minus the lowest CRPS on that prompt* — which re-bases a small difference\n54\tonto a much smaller number. A tenth of a percent of CRPS can therefore move a position by a\n55\thundred places in either direction. That makes position a loud, unstable readout of a tiny change:\n56\tuseful for checking you are in the right regime, useless as a target. Two runs of the same model\n57\tcan land far apart on it without differing in any way that matters.\n58\t\n59\tSo: **judge every experiment on CRPS**, per configuration and per asset. Report position if you\n60\tlike, but never trade a CRPS regression for a better one.\n61\t\n62\t**Your champion will also mine live, where it is paid in emission.** After the campaign, champions\n63\tare deployed as miners on the subnet, and emission rewards two things a mean CRPS hides:\n64\t\n65\t- **Every prompt answered.** A request that times out, raises, or returns a wrong path count is a\n66\t  miss, and a miss is scored like the field's 95th percentile on that prompt.\n67\t- **Contexts rougher than the snapshot.** A live context can hold longer gaps, and fewer real days\n68\t  than your `CONTEXT_DAYS`: some venues serve only their last 3 days of minutes, so on a new miner\n69\t  the oldest days of a longer context can be NaN.\n70\t\n71\tNone of this makes position a target: emission is earned by a low CRPS on every prompt, with no\n72\texceptions.\n73\t\n74\tHow the reported position is formed, for completeness: the aggregation does not average across\n75\tassets. It weights each asset by `ASSET_COEFFICIENTS` (`synth.validator.moving_average`),\n76\tnormalizes per miner, then applies a softmax, so a high-coefficient asset carries much more\n77\tweight. Your champion is measured over the **10 days following your data cutoff**, with each of\n78\tthe three configurations contributing one third regardless of asset count. The moving average\n79\tbackfills a model that appears late with the worst value in the set, so generation starts one\n80\thorizon before the window and yours is never treated as late.\n81\t\n82\t## Your resources\n83\t\n84\t- **Your workspace**: a standalone git repo. `agent/` contains a minimal starter model\n85\t  (`modeling.py`, log-normal martingale — REPLACE IT), an evaluator (`predict.py`) that you can improve, and\n86\t  `pyproject.toml` with pinned dependencies. First action: `uv sync`. Then run EVERY python\n87\t  command through `uv run` (`uv run python -c ...`, `uv run agent/predict.py`): the container's\n88\t  system `python3` has no numpy or pandas — only the workspace venv does.\n89\t  `agent/predict.py --days N --eval-end D` answers the prompts the validator scored in the N days\n90\t  before D, at the start times it actually used, read from the offline field bundle: the same\n91\t  kind of prompts your champion is scored on. `--eval-end` may be any day up to your cutoff, and\n92\t  each run scores only its own predictions, so runs can go in parallel. It is also used for the 1h\n93\t  format (pass `--time-length 3600 --time-increment 60`). `--cadence-minutes N` answers an even\n94\t  grid instead; a cadence that divides an hour puts every prompt on the round hour, where\n95\t  volatility spikes, so do not read an absolute calibration level off such a grid. `--crps-only`\n96\t  scores CRPS alone, against the snapshot's own prices, on such a grid and on any window of the\n97\t  snapshot, before the field bundle's span included: no field, no rank. Use it to compare models\n98\t  on the same grid.\n99\t  A field-relative run ranks you the way the validator's moving average does, over each\n100\t  competition's `window_days` of reward rounds. In a window's first `window_days`, that average\n101\t  would read scores from before the window, so `backtest()` drops those rounds: `--days N` ranks\n102\t  only the rounds after them. With N no longer than `window_days`, nothing is dropped, and the\n103\t  ranks rest on a truncated history. DATA.md gives each competition's `window_days` and the first\n104\t  round the bundle can rank.\n105\t- **`market_data/`**: a frozen snapshot of minute OHLCV (parquet partitions\n106\t  `prices/{ASSET}/1m/date=YYYY-MM-DD.parquet`, columns `open, high, low, close, volume,\n107\t  trade_count`, resolve it with\n108\t  `synth_lib.preparation.config.default_store_root`, do not hardcode either) covering\n109\t  **2025-08-01 → 2026-11-08**, mounted\n110\t  READ-ONLY, at the ROOT of the workspace. This is your ONLY source of market data. **There is no\n111\t  download step and no `prepare.py`**: the data is already in place, complete over that window,\n112\t  and the mount is read-only. Fetching prices is forbidden (see hard rules).\n113\t\n114\t  Each series is the venue the asset is actually SCORED against — Binance spot for BTC/ETH/SOL/\n115\t  XRP, Hyperliquid for everything else — one instrument per asset over its whole history. The\n116\t  history reaches back to each market's genuine FIRST TRADE, so depth varies by asset:\n117\t\n118\t  | From | Assets |\n119\t  |---|---|\n120\t  | 2025-08-01 | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE |\n121\t  | 2025-11-12 → 11-21 | NVDAX, TSLAX, GOOGLX, AAPLX |\n122\t  | 2025-12-22 / 2026-01-06 | XAU / WTIOIL |\n123\t  | 2026-03-18 / 2026-05-17 | SP500 / SPCX |\n124\t\n125\t  **Read `DATA.md` at the workspace root FIRST**: it is generated from this exact snapshot at\n126\t  setup and carries the measured truth — the partition schema, the minute semantics (a minute\n127\t  without a trade is a flat candle with `trade_count` 0, NOT market hours; NaN close = no price\n128\t  at all; missing file = before the market's first trade, or before the snapshot's first day),\n129\t  and a per-asset coverage table with density by era. Two caveats it quantifies that will bite if\n130\t  ignored: the early months of the newer Hyperliquid listings are genuinely THIN (low realized\n131\t  volatility there is real, not smoothing), and these are venue-specific perpetual prices with\n132\t  their own basis (xyz:SP500 trades around 7,500 — not the cash index).\n133\t\n134\t  **The venue never closes; the underlying does.** Minutes exist around the clock for every asset,\n135\t  so there is no session gap in the DATA — but the equity and commodity perps track markets that\n136\t  shut, and their VOLATILITY follows those hours closely. Measured on the snapshot: realized\n137\t  volatility per minute swings about 4-5x across the UTC day for the tokenised equities and gold,\n138\t  peaking at the US cash open and bottoming overnight, and weekdays run 2-4x hotter than weekends.\n139\t  The crypto assets, genuinely 24/7, are far flatter on both. A constant per-asset volatility is\n140\t  therefore too wide overnight and too narrow at the open, and CRPS charges for both. Measure the\n141\t  seasonality yourself before you model it, and check whether it holds for each asset rather than\n142\t  assuming one shape fits the group.\n143\t\n144\t  **Those hours keep local time; UTC does not.** The US session and the US releases (08:30, 10:30\n145\t  and 14:00 in New York) are fixed in New York time, and European hours in London time, so in UTC\n146\t  they move an hour at each daylight-saving switch. Your snapshot holds both regimes, and DATA.md\n147\t  names the switches inside it and the next one after your cutoff. A UTC-hour profile fitted on all\n148\t  of it smears the open over two hours; one fitted on summer months sits an hour early in winter.\n149\t  Keying a profile on New York or London time (`tz_convert`) is possible. Measure which clock each\n150\t  asset follows, on both regimes, before you choose.\n151\t\n152\t  Do not \"repair\" NaN gaps by interpolating; scoring already drops NaN per prompt, and inventing\n153\t  prices in the gaps is how you fool your own validation.\n154\t\n155\t  **Fitting vs comparing**: the FULL price history is yours to fit, train and calibrate on.\n156\t  FIELD-RELATIVE numbers start on each competition's first ranked round in DATA.md: **2026-08-03**\n157\t  for CRYPTO_24H and COM_EQU_24H, a few days earlier for CRYPTO_1H, whose moving average is\n158\t  shorter. From those rounds on, the field was scored on the prices your store holds, and so were\n159\t  all the rounds its moving average covers; the competitions and assets are today's, and\n160\t  COM_EQU_24H's scores have no holes. Never compute one on a window before them. Before them, and\n161\t  back to each asset's first day, `agent/predict.py --crps-only` scores CRPS alone: that is how you\n162\t  test a change on older regimes.\n163\t\n164\t  **Changes in the subnet's history, and what each means for a backtest across it:**\n165\t\n166\t  | Date | What changed |\n167\t  |---|---|\n168\t  | 2026-03-11 | The crypto-1h CRPS formula changed. Ranks before it are not comparable to today's. |\n169\t  | 2026-06-23 | The subnet split into the 3 current competitions. Before it: 2 profiles, and COM_EQU_24H did not exist. A field-relative rank spanning this date mixes incompatible reward structures — `synth_lib` will warn, and the number it gives you is meaningless. |\n170\t  | 2026-07-23 | The validator stopped scoring on Pyth prices: BTC, ETH, SOL and XRP moved to Binance spot, the others to Hyperliquid. Before it, the field was scored on prices your store does not hold. SPYX was retired the same day and SP500 introduced as a NEW asset, carrying the HIGHEST coefficient (3.44) in COM_EQU_24H; SP500's scored history starts here. Fit SP500 on its whole price series, and spend nothing on SPYX. |\n171\t  | 2026-09-04 | An outlier cap, in every competition: a CRPS above 10x the prompt's median is capped and ranked last. The backtester replays it by each prompt's scored time, so ranks across it stay comparable; CRPS itself is unaffected. |\n172\t  | 2026-09-22 | crypto-1h scoring added a volatility term. A crypto-1h CRPS from before it is not comparable with one after; `agent/predict.py --latest-formula` scores earlier prompts with it too, which keeps CRPS comparable (field ranks before the date then are not). |\n173\t  | 2026-10-28 | The prompt cadence changed. COM_EQU_24H's scored prompts went from about 24 a day per asset to 10, at the same times every day (DATA.md lists them), so a backtest after it samples a different mix of start times than one before it. |\n174\t\n175\t  **Loader expectations**: synth-lib's strict `load_range` RAISES on any NaN or missing day in\n176\t  the requested range — that is a feature for scoring, a trap for exploration. `agent/predict.py`\n177\t  shows the sanctioned lenient pattern; prior agents each rediscovered this at their own expense.\n178\t\n179\t  **Always run your commands from the workspace root**: synth-lib's backtester resolves the\n180\t  price store relative to the current directory (`MinutePriceStore(asset)` with no root, see\n181\t  `_price_store`). From a subfolder it would find nothing and would try to download the missing\n182\t  days — which the read-only mount refuses. `predict.py` already forces this directory and\n183\t  checks coverage before scoring; if you write your own evaluation script, reproduce these two\n184\t  safeguards.\n185\t- **`market_data/calendar/events.parquet`** (its path is in `$SYNTH_BENCHMARK_CALENDAR`): scheduled\n186\t  events — US macro releases (CPI, NFP, PPI, PCE, retail sales, GDP), FOMC statements, NYSE closures\n187\t  and early closes, the EIA weekly petroleum report, and the earnings of the companies behind the\n188\t  tokenised stocks — with every date published before your cutoff, upcoming ones included. DATA.md\n189\t  gives its columns, its span, the count of each type and which share a release minute. Your miner\n190\t  gets the same file in production, refreshed daily: read it inside `simulate()` through the env\n191\t  var, never from a copy or a hardcoded path, and expect an event type or a date to be missing.\n192\t- **synth-lib** (installed; public source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-lib): the official\n193\t  backtester — `from synth_lib.backtester.orchestration import backtest` — which computes CRPS with\n194\t  the validator's exact CRPS function, and can also place you among the recorded field. Use it\n195\t  via `agent/predict.py`; NEVER reimplement the metric. The backtester runs FULLY OFFLINE here:\n196\t  `SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT` is set in your environment and points at a bundle of\n197\t  historical scores inside your snapshot, so no API call is needed. DATA.md gives the measured\n198\t  span of scored prompts it holds: a field-relative number outside that span has nothing to\n199\t  compare against, and `agent/predict.py --crps-only` scores CRPS alone. DATA.md also gives the\n200\t  span of the cached realized paths, which are a different thing — they stand in for minutes your\n201\t  price store is missing.\n202\t- **synth-subnet** (a synth-lib dependency; source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-subnet): the\n203\t  validator's code. Read it to understand exactly how you'll be scored.\n204\t- **Public Synth API** (api.synthdata.co): historical field scores\n205\t  (`/validation/scores/historical`, query windows ≤ 2 days) and rewards (`/rewards/scores`).\n206\t  `to` names a whole UTC day, inclusive: a query to 08-16 also returns 08-16's rows.\n207\t  synth-lib handles pagination for you. Studying the field (CRPS levels, what the top miners do)\n208\t  is ALLOWED and recommended; copying the paths served by an individual miner is\n209\t  FORBIDDEN.\n210\t- **Miner dashboard analytics API** (`https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1`, schema at\n211\t  `https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1/openapi.json`): the public backend behind the miner\n212\t  dashboard — scoreboards, leaderboards, reward pools. Read the OpenAPI schema to discover the\n213\t  endpoints. Same rule as the scores API: studying the field is encouraged, copying an\n214\t  individual miner's served paths is not.\n215\t- **Pretrained models & Hugging Face**: allowed and in scope. The sandbox has network access to\n216\t  the HF Hub and PyPI; the GPU is yours and sits idle unless you use it. Time-series foundation\n217\t  models, fine-tuning, or classical ML are all legitimate strategies — as is deciding, with\n218\t  evidence, that they don't improve on closed-form calibration. If you go this way: download weights\n219\t  EARLY (bandwidth is part of your clock), set `HF_HOME` inside your workspace, and remember\n220\t  your champion must run OFFLINE — weights must be committed to your git workspace, total under\n221\t  a few GB (the harness collects your repo as a git bundle).\n222\t- **Serving latency**: the live validator gives you roughly the prompt's lead time (~1 minute)\n223\t  to respond — that is the hard bound. But the evaluation of your champion generates several\n224\t  thousand prompts, and your own backtests will too: at more than a few seconds per prompt\n225\t  (1000 paths, this hardware), your iteration loop and your evaluation both become the\n226\t  bottleneck. Measure your per-prompt latency early and treat ~5 s as the practical ceiling.\n227\t- **The web**: open for knowledge — searching the literature (arXiv, papers, reference\n228\t  implementations) is encouraged — but NEVER for fetching prices or market data (see hard\n229\t  rules).\n230\t\n231\t## Your envelope\n232\t\n233\t- Credit budget: 100.0 USD. Deadline: 18 h of wall-clock time.\n234\t- The `BUDGET.md` file in your workspace is maintained by the harness: READ IT AT THE START OF\n235\t  EACH ITERATION. When it contains a LANDING ORDER, it is non-negotiable.\n236\t- Hardware: GCP n1-standard-16: 16 vCPU / 60GB RAM / 1x NVIDIA T4 (16GB VRAM; Turing, no bf16). Your sandbox gets 14 CPU, 48GB RAM and the GPU. torch is already in your pyproject — no need to add it. ONLY ONE GPU job at a time; cap the memory of your heavy jobs.\n237\t- **Parallel work shares this envelope.** Every agent or job you start — subagents, workflows,\n238\t  background runs — spends from the same credit budget and runs on the same machine. The budget in\n239\t  `BUDGET.md` and the hardware above are totals, whatever a tool's own guidance says about token\n240\t  cost.\n241\t- **You are expected to use your envelope.** Iterating is the job: measure, change one thing,\n242\t  measure again. **Nominating is final** — the first time you stop while `agent/CHAMPION`\n243\t  exists, your campaign is over: you will not be resumed, and every remaining dollar and hour\n244\t  is forfeited. Until then you may keep iterating and amend your nomination. Nominate early\n245\t  only when your journal contains EVIDENCE that further iteration would not improve your Score.\n246\t  **A champion is MANDATORY.** What is scored is how strong your champion is, not whether you dared\n247\t  to nominate one: ending with no `agent/CHAMPION` is a Score of 0 and a wasted leg — strictly worse\n248\t  than nominating something imperfect.\n249\t\n250\t## Constraints (they are what make the result valid)\n251\t\n252\t1. **No-lookahead**: a prediction at `start_time` uses only information available at\n253\t   `start_time`. Your champion is evaluated on the 10 days FOLLOWING your data cutoff: treat\n254\t   everything after **2026-11-08** as nonexistent. Querying ANY API (scores, rewards,\n255\t   dashboard) for times after 2026-11-08 is lookahead into your own evaluation window and\n256\t   invalidates the result. Audited on your transcript.\n257\t2. **Prices = snapshot only.** No fetching market data online (price APIs, exchanges,\n258\t   aggregators). The web is for knowledge only. Audited on your transcript.\n259\t   Event dates come only from that calendar: a table typed from memory or fetched from the web goes\n260\t   stale and wrong where your miner serves.\n261\t3. **Scoring = synth-lib.** Every CRPS evaluation goes through `synth_lib.backtester`\n262\t   (via `agent/predict.py`). One implementation of the metric is what makes results comparable.\n263\t4. Not allowed: falsifying the journal or results; reproducing another participant's served\n264\t   output as your own.\n265\t5. **Keep binary and encoded data out of your terminal output.** Read parquet with pandas and\n266\t   `.npy` with numpy — never `cat` a parquet, a git bundle, or model weights, and never pipe\n267\t   base64 into your context. A screenful of encoded bytes buys you nothing, costs context, and\n268\t   is a known trigger for content classifiers that will end your turn.\n269\t6. **Start from public sources only**: synth-lib, synth-subnet, the Synth API, and your own\n270\t   ideas. The operator's existing miner models are not part of your materials and must not\n271\t   inform your work. Nothing of that kind belongs in your environment; if any of it turns up\n272\t   there, leave it unread and note it in `suggestions.md`.\n273\t7. Your final champion must expose, in `agent/modeling.py`:\n274\t   `simulate(asset, start_time, time_increment, time_length, num_simulations, context_prices)`\n275\t   -> tuple `(start_timestamp, time_increment, *paths)` — each path: a list of\n276\t   `time_length//time_increment + 1` floats starting at the last price in the context.\n277\t   **The `context_prices` contract, exactly**: a minute-indexed pd.DataFrame over the\n278\t   `CONTEXT_DAYS` days preceding `start_time`, with columns `open, high, low, close, volume,\n279\t   trade_count`. `close` is the series you are scored against; the rest are context. It MAY\n280\t   CONTAIN NaN in every column, on whole days before an asset's first trade or before the\n281\t   snapshot's first day too — your `simulate()` must tolerate them (drop, never interpolate).\n282\t   Evaluation passes the raw frame: handle gaps inside your model, not in a loader you hope\n283\t   evaluation shares.\n284\t   **`CONTEXT_DAYS` is yours to choose**, and to measure like any other parameter: a plain\n285\t   integer at module level in `agent/modeling.py`, from 3 to 30, not computed (a module without\n286\t   it gets 7). `agent/predict.py`, the interface check, the scoring and the live miner all read\n287\t   it, and any other value fails every one of them. One value serves every competition: slice a\n288\t   shorter context inside `simulate()` where you want one. The interface check reports the wall\n289\t   time of one call at your length.\n290\t   Runnable OFFLINE (weights/artifacts committed or included in the workspace).\n291\t8. **If your model uses randomness at all, seed it from `SYNTH_BENCHMARK_SEED`.** Nothing\n292\t   here asks you to introduce randomness — a deterministic champion ignores this rule. If you\n293\t   do use it, read the variable once at the top of `simulate()` and seed every generator your\n294\t   model touches from it: Python's `random`, NumPy, torch, and anything carrying its own. It\n295\t   is absent during your own runs and set only at evaluation, so fall back to a seed of your\n296\t   choosing when it is missing. What you may never do is hardcode a seed that ignores the\n297\t   variable, or leave a generator unseeded so the clock or OS entropy supplies it.\n298\t\n299\t## Required deliverables (in `agent/`)\n300\t\n301\tWrite `journal.md`, `report.md`, and `suggestions.md` in English — these reports are published\n302\tto the Synth miner community.\n303\t\n304\t- `journal.md`: one entry per iteration — Hypothesis / Change / Result (numbers) / Verdict\n305\t  (keep-discard) / Lesson. As you go: this is your memory if you crash.\n306\t- `report.md`: your final report, explainer style (audience = Synth miners). Sections: context,\n307\t  approach, experiments (failures included), results, limitations, what you'd do with more\n308\t  budget.\n309\t- `CHAMPION`: YAML — `sha` (an EXISTING git commit in your workspace, not a placeholder),\n310\t  `agent_dir: agent`, `profiles`, `notes`. `profiles` must list the formats your champion\n311\t  supports: `[low, high]` if you cover both 24h AND 1h (expected), `[low]` if you could only\n312\t  handle the 24h. `notes` MUST include your champion's backtested **CRPS per configuration**,\n313\t  produced by the official backtester (`agent/predict.py`) at 1000 paths, alongside the CRPS of\n314\t  whatever you started from — a nomination without measured numbers is incomplete. The harness checks the interface as soon as\n315\t  this file appears (result in BUDGET.md) — fix it if the check fails.\n316\t- `suggestions.md`: anything that falls outside your scope.\n317\t- you must commit all deliverables to your workspace git repo before the deadline.\n318\t\n319\t## Method (your choice, but recommended)\n320\t\n321\tYou choose your own research questions, hypotheses, splits, and criteria, and your own in-sample\n322\tand out-of-sample windows: there is no holdout rule, and the field bundle covers the span DATA.md\n323\tgives. What works here:\n324\twrite the hypothesis BEFORE the experiment, pre-registered kill thresholds, validation on 2\n325\twindows of opposite regimes (within the limits of the snapshot), one variable at a time,\n326\tfrequent commits. Watch out for the edges of eval windows: only backtest windows fully covered\n327\tby the snapshot. Lessons earlier agents paid for:\n328\t\n329\t- **Explain your CRPS before you tune it.** Break a backtest's CRPS down by asset, configuration,\n330\t  hour of day and day, and read the worst prompts: the realized path, your fan, and the field's CRPS\n331\t  on the same prompt (`backtest()` returns every miner's CRPS per prompt in `prompt_df`; save it from\n332\t  `agent/predict.py`). For each anomaly, find the explanation: a gap in the context, an event, a\n333\t  regime change, a bug in your model. Write it in your journal before you change anything. An\n334\t  explained anomaly tells you what to fix; a sweep over an unexplained one tunes to noise.\n335\t- **Price a residual before fitting it.** For a Gaussian fan, expected CRPS at scale σ against\n336\t  the right σ* is about 1 + ¼·(ln σ/σ*)² times the optimum: a ±20 % global scale error costs about\n337\t  1 %, and a width correction across buckets can gain at most about ¼ of the variance of the\n338\t  log width ratios. A minute of arithmetic tells you whether a day of sweeping can pay.\n339\t- **Test a feature by what it adds to a fast EWMA of realized volatility**, not by its own\n340\t  predictive power. Range estimators, volume, trade counts and the leverage effect act on future\n341\t  volatility through realized volatility, which the EWMA has already priced. What it cannot hold\n342\t  is information outside the context: the calendar, and anything older than the `CONTEXT_DAYS`\n343\t  `simulate()` receives.\n344\t- **Validate an event effect leave-one-event-out.** A type occurs 8 to 12 times a year, so a\n345\t  multiplier fitted and tested on the same releases is in-sample.\n346\t- **Check what `simulate()` can see before building on it**: one asset's `CONTEXT_DAYS` context\n347\t  per call, nothing else. A feature that needs another asset's current prices cannot be served.\n348\t- **Count the asset-window cells where a change helps**, across disjoint windows, not only its\n349\t  average: a small gain signed the same way in most cells survives; a larger one that flips sign\n350\t  between windows is noise.\n351\t- **Do not tune on the last weeks before your cutoff alone.** They hold one regime, and your\n352\t  champion is scored on the weeks after the cutoff, which may hold another. Validate a change on\n353\t  windows from different regimes across the whole span, and keep it only if it holds on them.\n354\t\n355\t## Resuming after a crash\n356\t\n357\tIf you restart with no context: reread this file, then `BUDGET.md`, then your `journal.md`,\n358\tand continue where you left off.\n359\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b5de06de-0f9b-444f-a451-a2a51a4613ad","uuid":"a0729d69-f519-4a39-8583-1ab2ceceded4","timestamp":"2026-11-11T13:13:14.576Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/agent/CAMPAIGN.md","content":"# CAMPAIGN campaign-7 — constitution (fable-5-2)\n\nYou are a research engineer working this problem on your own. No one is available to answer\nquestions while the run is in progress, so make the call yourself and record it in your journal.\nKeep working until the landing order arrives. What you are working on:\n\n> A calibration problem: build the price-simulation model with the lowest CRPS you can reach. CRPS measures how well your predicted distribution matches what actually happened, and lower is better. Your model must handle all three scoring configurations (CRYPTO_24H, COM_EQU_24H, CRYPTO_1H) and is measured over the 10 days following your data cutoff. Judge every experiment on CRPS; the constitution explains why the field-relative position is a poor target. Choose your own research questions. Find something publishable — honest failures count too.\n\n## Context: Synth (Bittensor SN50)\n\nSynth is a Bittensor subnet where \"miners\" serve probabilistic price simulations: for a given\nasset and start time, a miner returns N simulated price paths (a \"fan\"). A validator then\ncompares these paths against the actually observed prices and scores each miner on **CRPS**\n(Continuous Ranked Probability Score — lower = better), summed across several horizons. A good\nminer produces well-calibrated distributions: neither too narrow (overconfidence is punished)\nnor too wide (imprecision is punished).\n\n## The three scoring configurations\n\nThe subnet scores three configurations across two horizons (its code calls them *competitions*;\n`synth.validator.competition_config` is the source of truth). Your model has to handle all three —\na `simulate()` that ignores `time_increment`/`time_length` and assumes a 24h horizon breaks on\ncrypto-1h.\n\n| Competition | Assets | Horizon | Step | Points/path |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CRYPTO_24H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 24h (86400 s) | 5min (300 s) | 289 |\n| COM_EQU_24H | XAU, NVDAX, GOOGLX, TSLAX, AAPLX, WTIOIL, SPCX, SP500 | 24h | 5min | 289 |\n| CRYPTO_1H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 1h (3600 s) | 1min (60 s) | 61 |\n\nBTC, ETH, SOL, XRP and HYPE are in two competitions: look one up by `(asset, time_length)`\n(`synth_lib.backtester.config.competition_for`), never by asset. A 24h prompt scored with the\ncrypto-1h intervals is wrong by a large, silent margin.\n\n**1000 simulations per prompt** in all three cases (`PromptConfig.num_simulations`); a response\nwhose path count differs is rejected outright by the validator\n(`synth.validator.response_validation_v2`). `simulate()` receives `num_simulations` as an\nargument — honour whatever it is given, never hardcode a path count. Lower it only to speed up\nyour own iteration loops, never in what your champion serves.\n\nScoring differs substantially between the two horizons.\nThe exact definition (source of truth): `synth.validator.competition_config` and\n`synth.validator.crps_calculation` in the synth-subnet repo. Read them.\n\n## What you are optimising\n\n**CRPS is the objective. Lower is better.** It is the only quantity that measures your model\ndirectly: it compares your predicted distribution against what actually happened, prompt by\nprompt, and it does not depend on what anyone else submitted.\n\nA field-relative position is also reported, and it is tempting to optimise because it looks like a\nresult. **Do not.** The submissions are tightly packed, and the per-prompt score the aggregation\nconsumes is your CRPS *minus the lowest CRPS on that prompt* — which re-bases a small difference\nonto a much smaller number. A tenth of a percent of CRPS can therefore move a position by a\nhundred places in either direction. That makes position a loud, unstable readout of a tiny change:\nuseful for checking you are in the right regime, useless as a target. Two runs of the same model\ncan land far apart on it without differing in any way that matters.\n\nSo: **judge every experiment on CRPS**, per configuration and per asset. Report position if you\nlike, but never trade a CRPS regression for a better one.\n\n**Your champion will also mine live, where it is paid in emission.** After the campaign, champions\nare deployed as miners on the subnet, and emission rewards two things a mean CRPS hides:\n\n- **Every prompt answered.** A request that times out, raises, or returns a wrong path count is a\n  miss, and a miss is scored like the field's 95th percentile on that prompt.\n- **Contexts rougher than the snapshot.** A live context can hold longer gaps, and fewer real days\n  than your `CONTEXT_DAYS`: some venues serve only their last 3 days of minutes, so on a new miner\n  the oldest days of a longer context can be NaN.\n\nNone of this makes position a target: emission is earned by a low CRPS on every prompt, with no\nexceptions.\n\nHow the reported position is formed, for completeness: the aggregation does not average across\nassets. It weights each asset by `ASSET_COEFFICIENTS` (`synth.validator.moving_average`),\nnormalizes per miner, then applies a softmax, so a high-coefficient asset carries much more\nweight. Your champion is measured over the **10 days following your data cutoff**, with each of\nthe three configurations contributing one third regardless of asset count. The moving average\nbackfills a model that appears late with the worst value in the set, so generation starts one\nhorizon before the window and yours is never treated as late.\n\n## Your resources\n\n- **Your workspace**: a standalone git repo. `agent/` contains a minimal starter model\n  (`modeling.py`, log-normal martingale — REPLACE IT), an evaluator (`predict.py`) that you can improve, and\n  `pyproject.toml` with pinned dependencies. First action: `uv sync`. Then run EVERY python\n  command through `uv run` (`uv run python -c ...`, `uv run agent/predict.py`): the container's\n  system `python3` has no numpy or pandas — only the workspace venv does.\n  `agent/predict.py --days N --eval-end D` answers the prompts the validator scored in the N days\n  before D, at the start times it actually used, read from the offline field bundle: the same\n  kind of prompts your champion is scored on. `--eval-end` may be any day up to your cutoff, and\n  each run scores only its own predictions, so runs can go in parallel. It is also used for the 1h\n  format (pass `--time-length 3600 --time-increment 60`). `--cadence-minutes N` answers an even\n  grid instead; a cadence that divides an hour puts every prompt on the round hour, where\n  volatility spikes, so do not read an absolute calibration level off such a grid. `--crps-only`\n  scores CRPS alone, against the snapshot's own prices, on such a grid and on any window of the\n  snapshot, before the field bundle's span included: no field, no rank. Use it to compare models\n  on the same grid.\n  A field-relative run ranks you the way the validator's moving average does, over each\n  competition's `window_days` of reward rounds. In a window's first `window_days`, that average\n  would read scores from before the window, so `backtest()` drops those rounds: `--days N` ranks\n  only the rounds after them. With N no longer than `window_days`, nothing is dropped, and the\n  ranks rest on a truncated history. DATA.md gives each competition's `window_days` and the first\n  round the bundle can rank.\n- **`market_data/`**: a frozen snapshot of minute OHLCV (parquet partitions\n  `prices/{ASSET}/1m/date=YYYY-MM-DD.parquet`, columns `open, high, low, close, volume,\n  trade_count`, resolve it with\n  `synth_lib.preparation.config.default_store_root`, do not hardcode either) covering\n  **2025-08-01 → 2026-11-08**, mounted\n  READ-ONLY, at the ROOT of the workspace. This is your ONLY source of market data. **There is no\n  download step and no `prepare.py`**: the data is already in place, complete over that window,\n  and the mount is read-only. Fetching prices is forbidden (see hard rules).\n\n  Each series is the venue the asset is actually SCORED against — Binance spot for BTC/ETH/SOL/\n  XRP, Hyperliquid for everything else — one instrument per asset over its whole history. The\n  history reaches back to each market's genuine FIRST TRADE, so depth varies by asset:\n\n  | From | Assets |\n  |---|---|\n  | 2025-08-01 | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE |\n  | 2025-11-12 → 11-21 | NVDAX, TSLAX, GOOGLX, AAPLX |\n  | 2025-12-22 / 2026-01-06 | XAU / WTIOIL |\n  | 2026-03-18 / 2026-05-17 | SP500 / SPCX |\n\n  **Read `DATA.md` at the workspace root FIRST**: it is generated from this exact snapshot at\n  setup and carries the measured truth — the partition schema, the minute semantics (a minute\n  without a trade is a flat candle with `trade_count` 0, NOT market hours; NaN close = no price\n  at all; missing file = before the market's first trade, or before the snapshot's first day),\n  and a per-asset coverage table with density by era. Two caveats it quantifies that will bite if\n  ignored: the early months of the newer Hyperliquid listings are genuinely THIN (low realized\n  volatility there is real, not smoothing), and these are venue-specific perpetual prices with\n  their own basis (xyz:SP500 trades around 7,500 — not the cash index).\n\n  **The venue never closes; the underlying does.** Minutes exist around the clock for every asset,\n  so there is no session gap in the DATA — but the equity and commodity perps track markets that\n  shut, and their VOLATILITY follows those hours closely. Measured on the snapshot: realized\n  volatility per minute swings about 4-5x across the UTC day for the tokenised equities and gold,\n  peaking at the US cash open and bottoming overnight, and weekdays run 2-4x hotter than weekends.\n  The crypto assets, genuinely 24/7, are far flatter on both. A constant per-asset volatility is\n  therefore too wide overnight and too narrow at the open, and CRPS charges for both. Measure the\n  seasonality yourself before you model it, and check whether it holds for each asset rather than\n  assuming one shape fits the group.\n\n  **Those hours keep local time; UTC does not.** The US session and the US releases (08:30, 10:30\n  and 14:00 in New York) are fixed in New York time, and European hours in London time, so in UTC\n  they move an hour at each daylight-saving switch. Your snapshot holds both regimes, and DATA.md\n  names the switches inside it and the next one after your cutoff. A UTC-hour profile fitted on all\n  of it smears the open over two hours; one fitted on summer months sits an hour early in winter.\n  Keying a profile on New York or London time (`tz_convert`) is possible. Measure which clock each\n  asset follows, on both regimes, before you choose.\n\n  Do not \"repair\" NaN gaps by interpolating; scoring already drops NaN per prompt, and inventing\n  prices in the gaps is how you fool your own validation.\n\n  **Fitting vs comparing**: the FULL price history is yours to fit, train and calibrate on.\n  FIELD-RELATIVE numbers start on each competition's first ranked round in DATA.md: **2026-08-03**\n  for CRYPTO_24H and COM_EQU_24H, a few days earlier for CRYPTO_1H, whose moving average is\n  shorter. From those rounds on, the field was scored on the prices your store holds, and so were\n  all the rounds its moving average covers; the competitions and assets are today's, and\n  COM_EQU_24H's scores have no holes. Never compute one on a window before them. Before them, and\n  back to each asset's first day, `agent/predict.py --crps-only` scores CRPS alone: that is how you\n  test a change on older regimes.\n\n  **Changes in the subnet's history, and what each means for a backtest across it:**\n\n  | Date | What changed |\n  |---|---|\n  | 2026-03-11 | The crypto-1h CRPS formula changed. Ranks before it are not comparable to today's. |\n  | 2026-06-23 | The subnet split into the 3 current competitions. Before it: 2 profiles, and COM_EQU_24H did not exist. A field-relative rank spanning this date mixes incompatible reward structures — `synth_lib` will warn, and the number it gives you is meaningless. |\n  | 2026-07-23 | The validator stopped scoring on Pyth prices: BTC, ETH, SOL and XRP moved to Binance spot, the others to Hyperliquid. Before it, the field was scored on prices your store does not hold. SPYX was retired the same day and SP500 introduced as a NEW asset, carrying the HIGHEST coefficient (3.44) in COM_EQU_24H; SP500's scored history starts here. Fit SP500 on its whole price series, and spend nothing on SPYX. |\n  | 2026-09-04 | An outlier cap, in every competition: a CRPS above 10x the prompt's median is capped and ranked last. The backtester replays it by each prompt's scored time, so ranks across it stay comparable; CRPS itself is unaffected. |\n  | 2026-09-22 | crypto-1h scoring added a volatility term. A crypto-1h CRPS from before it is not comparable with one after; `agent/predict.py --latest-formula` scores earlier prompts with it too, which keeps CRPS comparable (field ranks before the date then are not). |\n  | 2026-10-28 | The prompt cadence changed. COM_EQU_24H's scored prompts went from about 24 a day per asset to 10, at the same times every day (DATA.md lists them), so a backtest after it samples a different mix of start times than one before it. |\n\n  **Loader expectations**: synth-lib's strict `load_range` RAISES on any NaN or missing day in\n  the requested range — that is a feature for scoring, a trap for exploration. `agent/predict.py`\n  shows the sanctioned lenient pattern; prior agents each rediscovered this at their own expense.\n\n  **Always run your commands from the workspace root**: synth-lib's backtester resolves the\n  price store relative to the current directory (`MinutePriceStore(asset)` with no root, see\n  `_price_store`). From a subfolder it would find nothing and would try to download the missing\n  days — which the read-only mount refuses. `predict.py` already forces this directory and\n  checks coverage before scoring; if you write your own evaluation script, reproduce these two\n  safeguards.\n- **`market_data/calendar/events.parquet`** (its path is in `$SYNTH_BENCHMARK_CALENDAR`): scheduled\n  events — US macro releases (CPI, NFP, PPI, PCE, retail sales, GDP), FOMC statements, NYSE closures\n  and early closes, the EIA weekly petroleum report, and the earnings of the companies behind the\n  tokenised stocks — with every date published before your cutoff, upcoming ones included. DATA.md\n  gives its columns, its span, the count of each type and which share a release minute. Your miner\n  gets the same file in production, refreshed daily: read it inside `simulate()` through the env\n  var, never from a copy or a hardcoded path, and expect an event type or a date to be missing.\n- **synth-lib** (installed; public source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-lib): the official\n  backtester — `from synth_lib.backtester.orchestration import backtest` — which computes CRPS with\n  the validator's exact CRPS function, and can also place you among the recorded field. Use it\n  via `agent/predict.py`; NEVER reimplement the metric. The backtester runs FULLY OFFLINE here:\n  `SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT` is set in your environment and points at a bundle of\n  historical scores inside your snapshot, so no API call is needed. DATA.md gives the measured\n  span of scored prompts it holds: a field-relative number outside that span has nothing to\n  compare against, and `agent/predict.py --crps-only` scores CRPS alone. DATA.md also gives the\n  span of the cached realized paths, which are a different thing — they stand in for minutes your\n  price store is missing.\n- **synth-subnet** (a synth-lib dependency; source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-subnet): the\n  validator's code. Read it to understand exactly how you'll be scored.\n- **Public Synth API** (api.synthdata.co): historical field scores\n  (`/validation/scores/historical`, query windows ≤ 2 days) and rewards (`/rewards/scores`).\n  `to` names a whole UTC day, inclusive: a query to 08-16 also returns 08-16's rows.\n  synth-lib handles pagination for you. Studying the field (CRPS levels, what the top miners do)\n  is ALLOWED and recommended; copying the paths served by an individual miner is\n  FORBIDDEN.\n- **Miner dashboard analytics API** (`https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1`, schema at\n  `https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1/openapi.json`): the public backend behind the miner\n  dashboard — scoreboards, leaderboards, reward pools. Read the OpenAPI schema to discover the\n  endpoints. Same rule as the scores API: studying the field is encouraged, copying an\n  individual miner's served paths is not.\n- **Pretrained models & Hugging Face**: allowed and in scope. The sandbox has network access to\n  the HF Hub and PyPI; the GPU is yours and sits idle unless you use it. Time-series foundation\n  models, fine-tuning, or classical ML are all legitimate strategies — as is deciding, with\n  evidence, that they don't improve on closed-form calibration. If you go this way: download weights\n  EARLY (bandwidth is part of your clock), set `HF_HOME` inside your workspace, and remember\n  your champion must run OFFLINE — weights must be committed to your git workspace, total under\n  a few GB (the harness collects your repo as a git bundle).\n- **Serving latency**: the live validator gives you roughly the prompt's lead time (~1 minute)\n  to respond — that is the hard bound. But the evaluation of your champion generates several\n  thousand prompts, and your own backtests will too: at more than a few seconds per prompt\n  (1000 paths, this hardware), your iteration loop and your evaluation both become the\n  bottleneck. Measure your per-prompt latency early and treat ~5 s as the practical ceiling.\n- **The web**: open for knowledge — searching the literature (arXiv, papers, reference\n  implementations) is encouraged — but NEVER for fetching prices or market data (see hard\n  rules).\n\n## Your envelope\n\n- Credit budget: 100.0 USD. Deadline: 18 h of wall-clock time.\n- The `BUDGET.md` file in your workspace is maintained by the harness: READ IT AT THE START OF\n  EACH ITERATION. When it contains a LANDING ORDER, it is non-negotiable.\n- Hardware: GCP n1-standard-16: 16 vCPU / 60GB RAM / 1x NVIDIA T4 (16GB VRAM; Turing, no bf16). Your sandbox gets 14 CPU, 48GB RAM and the GPU. torch is already in your pyproject — no need to add it. ONLY ONE GPU job at a time; cap the memory of your heavy jobs.\n- **Parallel work shares this envelope.** Every agent or job you start — subagents, workflows,\n  background runs — spends from the same credit budget and runs on the same machine. The budget in\n  `BUDGET.md` and the hardware above are totals, whatever a tool's own guidance says about token\n  cost.\n- **You are expected to use your envelope.** Iterating is the job: measure, change one thing,\n  measure again. **Nominating is final** — the first time you stop while `agent/CHAMPION`\n  exists, your campaign is over: you will not be resumed, and every remaining dollar and hour\n  is forfeited. Until then you may keep iterating and amend your nomination. Nominate early\n  only when your journal contains EVIDENCE that further iteration would not improve your Score.\n  **A champion is MANDATORY.** What is scored is how strong your champion is, not whether you dared\n  to nominate one: ending with no `agent/CHAMPION` is a Score of 0 and a wasted leg — strictly worse\n  than nominating something imperfect.\n\n## Constraints (they are what make the result valid)\n\n1. **No-lookahead**: a prediction at `start_time` uses only information available at\n   `start_time`. Your champion is evaluated on the 10 days FOLLOWING your data cutoff: treat\n   everything after **2026-11-08** as nonexistent. Querying ANY API (scores, rewards,\n   dashboard) for times after 2026-11-08 is lookahead into your own evaluation window and\n   invalidates the result. Audited on your transcript.\n2. **Prices = snapshot only.** No fetching market data online (price APIs, exchanges,\n   aggregators). The web is for knowledge only. Audited on your transcript.\n   Event dates come only from that calendar: a table typed from memory or fetched from the web goes\n   stale and wrong where your miner serves.\n3. **Scoring = synth-lib.** Every CRPS evaluation goes through `synth_lib.backtester`\n   (via `agent/predict.py`). One implementation of the metric is what makes results comparable.\n4. Not allowed: falsifying the journal or results; reproducing another participant's served\n   output as your own.\n5. **Keep binary and encoded data out of your terminal output.** Read parquet with pandas and\n   `.npy` with numpy — never `cat` a parquet, a git bundle, or model weights, and never pipe\n   base64 into your context. A screenful of encoded bytes buys you nothing, costs context, and\n   is a known trigger for content classifiers that will end your turn.\n6. **Start from public sources only**: synth-lib, synth-subnet, the Synth API, and your own\n   ideas. The operator's existing miner models are not part of your materials and must not\n   inform your work. Nothing of that kind belongs in your environment; if any of it turns up\n   there, leave it unread and note it in `suggestions.md`.\n7. Your final champion must expose, in `agent/modeling.py`:\n   `simulate(asset, start_time, time_increment, time_length, num_simulations, context_prices)`\n   -> tuple `(start_timestamp, time_increment, *paths)` — each path: a list of\n   `time_length//time_increment + 1` floats starting at the last price in the context.\n   **The `context_prices` contract, exactly**: a minute-indexed pd.DataFrame over the\n   `CONTEXT_DAYS` days preceding `start_time`, with columns `open, high, low, close, volume,\n   trade_count`. `close` is the series you are scored against; the rest are context. It MAY\n   CONTAIN NaN in every column, on whole days before an asset's first trade or before the\n   snapshot's first day too — your `simulate()` must tolerate them (drop, never interpolate).\n   Evaluation passes the raw frame: handle gaps inside your model, not in a loader you hope\n   evaluation shares.\n   **`CONTEXT_DAYS` is yours to choose**, and to measure like any other parameter: a plain\n   integer at module level in `agent/modeling.py`, from 3 to 30, not computed (a module without\n   it gets 7). `agent/predict.py`, the interface check, the scoring and the live miner all read\n   it, and any other value fails every one of them. One value serves every competition: slice a\n   shorter context inside `simulate()` where you want one. The interface check reports the wall\n   time of one call at your length.\n   Runnable OFFLINE (weights/artifacts committed or included in the workspace).\n8. **If your model uses randomness at all, seed it from `SYNTH_BENCHMARK_SEED`.** Nothing\n   here asks you to introduce randomness — a deterministic champion ignores this rule. If you\n   do use it, read the variable once at the top of `simulate()` and seed every generator your\n   model touches from it: Python's `random`, NumPy, torch, and anything carrying its own. It\n   is absent during your own runs and set only at evaluation, so fall back to a seed of your\n   choosing when it is missing. What you may never do is hardcode a seed that ignores the\n   variable, or leave a generator unseeded so the clock or OS entropy supplies it.\n\n## Required deliverables (in `agent/`)\n\nWrite `journal.md`, `report.md`, and `suggestions.md` in English — these reports are published\nto the Synth miner community.\n\n- `journal.md`: one entry per iteration — Hypothesis / Change / Result (numbers) / Verdict\n  (keep-discard) / Lesson. As you go: this is your memory if you crash.\n- `report.md`: your final report, explainer style (audience = Synth miners). Sections: context,\n  approach, experiments (failures included), results, limitations, what you'd do with more\n  budget.\n- `CHAMPION`: YAML — `sha` (an EXISTING git commit in your workspace, not a placeholder),\n  `agent_dir: agent`, `profiles`, `notes`. `profiles` must list the formats your champion\n  supports: `[low, high]` if you cover both 24h AND 1h (expected), `[low]` if you could only\n  handle the 24h. `notes` MUST include your champion's backtested **CRPS per configuration**,\n  produced by the official backtester (`agent/predict.py`) at 1000 paths, alongside the CRPS of\n  whatever you started from — a nomination without measured numbers is incomplete. The harness checks the interface as soon as\n  this file appears (result in BUDGET.md) — fix it if the check fails.\n- `suggestions.md`: anything that falls outside your scope.\n- you must commit all deliverables to your workspace git repo before the deadline.\n\n## Method (your choice, but recommended)\n\nYou choose your own research questions, hypotheses, splits, and criteria, and your own in-sample\nand out-of-sample windows: there is no holdout rule, and the field bundle covers the span DATA.md\ngives. What works here:\nwrite the hypothesis BEFORE the experiment, pre-registered kill thresholds, validation on 2\nwindows of opposite regimes (within the limits of the snapshot), one variable at a time,\nfrequent commits. Watch out for the edges of eval windows: only backtest windows fully covered\nby the snapshot. Lessons earlier agents paid for:\n\n- **Explain your CRPS before you tune it.** Break a backtest's CRPS down by asset, configuration,\n  hour of day and day, and read the worst prompts: the realized path, your fan, and the field's CRPS\n  on the same prompt (`backtest()` returns every miner's CRPS per prompt in `prompt_df`; save it from\n  `agent/predict.py`). For each anomaly, find the explanation: a gap in the context, an event, a\n  regime change, a bug in your model. Write it in your journal before you change anything. An\n  explained anomaly tells you what to fix; a sweep over an unexplained one tunes to noise.\n- **Price a residual before fitting it.** For a Gaussian fan, expected CRPS at scale σ against\n  the right σ* is about 1 + ¼·(ln σ/σ*)² times the optimum: a ±20 % global scale error costs about\n  1 %, and a width correction across buckets can gain at most about ¼ of the variance of the\n  log width ratios. A minute of arithmetic tells you whether a day of sweeping can pay.\n- **Test a feature by what it adds to a fast EWMA of realized volatility**, not by its own\n  predictive power. Range estimators, volume, trade counts and the leverage effect act on future\n  volatility through realized volatility, which the EWMA has already priced. What it cannot hold\n  is information outside the context: the calendar, and anything older than the `CONTEXT_DAYS`\n  `simulate()` receives.\n- **Validate an event effect leave-one-event-out.** A type occurs 8 to 12 times a year, so a\n  multiplier fitted and tested on the same releases is in-sample.\n- **Check what `simulate()` can see before building on it**: one asset's `CONTEXT_DAYS` context\n  per call, nothing else. A feature that needs another asset's current prices cannot be served.\n- **Count the asset-window cells where a change helps**, across disjoint windows, not only its\n  average: a small gain signed the same way in most cells survives; a larger one that flips sign\n  between windows is noise.\n- **Do not tune on the last weeks before your cutoff alone.** They hold one regime, and your\n  champion is scored on the weeks after the cutoff, which may hold another. Validate a change on\n  windows from different regimes across the whole span, and keep it only if it holds on them.\n\n## Resuming after a crash\n\nIf you restart with no context: reread this file, then `BUDGET.md`, then your `journal.md`,\nand continue where you left off.\n","numLines":359,"startLine":1,"totalLines":359}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CjTEKBCavf8yy5bnZVNxJN","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
